{
  "image_name": "3e74246f2c015e9ea54083933724872b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a smooth muscle cell can switch between two states, a contractile phenotype and a synthetic phenotype, with a reversible transition indicated between them. On the left, the contractile state is promoted by cues such as TGFβ and cell–cell contact. Inside the cell, MYOCD works with SRF at a CArG DNA element to activate contraction-related genes, representing the differentiated, force-generating program.\n\nOn the right, the synthetic phenotype is associated with a broader, more spread cell shape and a different regulatory network. Inflammatory cytokines, cholesterol, and PDGF are shown as external signals that shift gene control away from the contractile program. NF-κB and AP-1 appear to suppress or interfere with MYOCD, while KLF4 is recruited near a G/C element adjacent to CArG, suggesting transcriptional reprogramming. miR-143 and miR-145 are also included as part of the regulatory loop linked to MYOCD and KLF4.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that environmental and inflammatory signals can repress the MYOCD/SRF-driven contractile gene program and promote a synthetic, less contractile smooth muscle cell state.",
